title: Query DSL grammar
tags: Query
kind: reference
aliases: uuid uuid_in in
summary: The whole DSL grammar, and the rules its lexer and parser follow — keywords, strings, dates, uuid atoms, nesting.

```
query     := or_expr
or_expr   := and_expr ( "OR" and_expr )*
and_expr  := unary ( "AND" unary )*
unary     := "NOT" unary | atom
atom      := "(" query ")" | uuid | uuid_list | in_call | osm_call | same_call | predicate

predicate := field "IS" ( "PRESENT" | "ABSENT" | "UNKNOWN" )
           | field cmp_op literal
           | field "MATCHES" string
           | field "->"  ( string | "(" query ")" )
           | field "->*" ( string | "(" query ")" )
           | field "=>*" ( string | "(" query ")" )

osm_call  := ( "osm" | "osmd" ) "(" field "," string ")"
same_call := "same" "(" fieldname "," query ")"
uuid_list := "uuid_in" "(" [ uuid ( "," uuid )* [ "," ] ] ")"
in_call   := "in" "(" field ( "," literal )* [ "," ] ")"

cmp_op    := "=" | "!=" | "<" | "<=" | ">" | ">="
literal   := int | float | string | "true" | "false" | datetime
datetime  := "@" string | "@" int        # ISO-8601 string, or Unix milliseconds
field     := fieldname [ ":" aspect ]
aspect    := "raw" | "value" | "parent" | "path"
string    := '"' … '"'
uuid      := 32 ASCII hexadecimal characters
```

The parser is shared code (`metafolder_core::dsl::parse_query`), used by the CLI and
the GUI.

* ''Keywords'' (`AND`, `OR`, `NOT`, `IS`, `PRESENT`, `ABSENT`, `UNKNOWN`, `MATCHES`) are case-sensitive uppercase; `true`/`false` lowercase.
* ''Precedence'': `NOT` over `AND` over `OR`; parentheses override.
* ''Nesting'' is bounded at 128 levels — the parser is recursive, so nesting is stack depth, and the input comes straight from a user. Past it: "query too deeply nested".
* ''Strings'': `\"` and `\\` decode to the bare character; any other backslash escape is kept verbatim, so a `MATCHES` pattern carries regex escapes (`"\.config"`). A literal backslash in a regex is `\\\\`.
* ''Arrows'' `->`, `->*` and `=>*` are single tokens, lexed by maximal munch — `=>*` is never `=` followed by `>`. (`~` is the simplified language's `MATCHES` ([[The default grammar]]); the DSL refuses it.)
* ''Function-call operators'' — `osm`, `osmd`, `same`, `uuid_in`, `in` — are recognised only when immediately followed by `(`, so a field named `osm` or `same` stays usable (`osm = "x"`).
* ''`in(field, v1, v2, …)`'' is `field = v1 OR field = v2 OR …` as one node (`in` in the [[Query IR]]), the field's aspect included (`in(mfr_path:path, "/a", "/b")`). Like `uuid_in`, a trailing comma is accepted and `in(field)` is the empty set. An `OR` of `=` is not folded into it.
* ''A bare uuid'' standing where a predicate is expected is a predicate on the metarecord's identity (`uuid_in` with one element). One spelling only: the 32-hex form every service emits, case-insensitive. Hyphenated, braced or URN forms are not DSL (the JSON accepts them), and a quoted `"<32hex>"` is a string, not a uuid. Since a field name may itself be 32 hex characters, a one-token lookahead decides: followed by an operator it is a field (`abcdef…ab = 3`), otherwise a uuid. A hex run of 8 or more characters that is not exactly 32 is reported as a truncated uuid. Several bare uuids joined by `OR` fold into one `uuid_in`.
* ''Aspects'' are accepted on any predicate that reads a value; the daemon, which alone knows a field's type, enforces which aspects apply ([[Field aspects]]). In `osm`/`osmd`, `field:path` and `field:value` are accepted spellings of the two modes; a contradicting one (`osmd(f:path, …)`) is a parse error.
* ''Reference values have no literal'': a `refbase` or `externalref` field cannot be compared in the DSL — only through a JSON `eq`. Writing `origin -> (<uuid>)` against a `refbase` field returns no rows and no error, a known gap. A `ref` is compared by following it: `tag -> (<uuid>)`.
